comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-02T09:29:57.622Z","harness:claude-code","GH-1652 (opened 2026-11-02, reproduced against SDK 2026.11.2 on Node 26) maps here. pm-gh1647's post-open check correctly refuses with lock_path_unsafe when locks/ is swapped for a symlink between the confinement checks and the exclusive open. But the 0-byte <id>.lock that the open created stays in the symlink target, outside the tracker. This is the refusal-side half of the post-create entry seam (body item 3): the cleanup must be bound to the same verified directory and inode identity as the check, or it becomes a pathname unlink outside the tracker. Added as an acceptance criterion instead of filing a separate item, because the fix shares the dirfd binding this item already prescribes. Linux procfs dirfd: unlink only when the opened descriptor's dev/ino still matches the bound entry. Elsewhere: refuse, and report the residue path in the refusal context so the caller can remove it."
body: "Discovered from pm-gh1647 during the PR 1648 CodeRabbit review (two Major and two Minor follow-ups, all present at the merge base before pm-gh1647).\n\npm-gh1647 refuses a lock directory that is ALREADY a symlink or non-directory, and detects a swap between its confinement check and `open(wx)` by comparing dev/ino identity. Every later step still acts by pathname, so a concurrent writer that swaps `locks/` (or a parent) for a symlink in the window can make pm act outside the tracker:\n\n1. **`pm gc --scope locks` sweep** (`src/core/lock/lock-gc.ts`): `inspectConfinedLockDirectory` runs once before `readdir`. The per-entry `readFile`, the cleanup gate in `removeObservedLock` and `fs.unlink(lockPath)` all re-resolve the path. A mid-scan swap can unlink an expired `*.lock` in an outside directory and report it as `locks/<file>`.\n2. **Post-EEXIST stale reclaim** (`src/core/lock/lock.ts`, acquire loop): after `EEXIST`, `assertLockCreateContention` checks only that the final entry is not a symlink. The reclaim then reads `lockPath`, takes the stale cleanup gate and unlinks `lockPath` by pathname. If the directory was swapped before `open(wx)`, `EEXIST` can come from an outside `<id>.lock`, and a stale or dead-owner outside file can be deleted.\n3. **Post-create entry check** (`verifyConfinedLockCreation`): the directory identity is re-inspected by pathname and then `lstat(lockPath)` runs separately. A swap between the two calls to an outside directory that holds a hard link to the created inode passes the identity check. The returned lease then releases and fences by pathname.\n4. **lstat failure treated as absent** (`assertLockEntryNotSymlink`): every lstat error returns early, so a transient non-ENOENT failure after `EEXIST` lets `readLockInfo` read the entry with a symlink-following `readFile`. Windows `EPERM`/`EACCES`/`EBUSY` delete-pending waits (recognized by `isLockCreateContention`) must keep waiting rather than become failures.\n\nThreat model: a pre-existing symlink committed into a cloned repository is already refused by pm-gh1647. These windows need a concurrent local writer that can modify `.agents/pm/` while pm runs, which is the same actor that could edit items directly, so the impact is narrower. It still contradicts the documented confinement guarantee.\n\nApproach: Node has no `openat` / `unlinkat`. On Linux, open the verified directory with `O_DIRECTORY | O_NOFOLLOW` and address entries through `/proc/self/fd/<dirfd>/<name>`; the magic link stays bound to the verified directory inode even if the path is swapped, which gives `*at` semantics for read, gate creation and unlink. A pathname re-check is NOT a fix, because a writer can swap the directory between the check and the unlink. Where a directory-bound operation is unavailable (no procfs, other platforms), refuse the destructive step (typed `lock_path_unsafe` / `lock_directory_replaced`) rather than proceeding by pathname: `pm gc --scope locks` refuses to sweep, stale reclaim refuses and waits or fails with the typed refusal, and creation verification refuses when it cannot bind the entry check."
